    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\n810,592 Honda and Acura Vehicles Recalled Over Takata-Produced Airbag Inflators\n55 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nToyota Motor North America recently announced that it's recalling 314,029 vehicles in the United States of America, namely older cars, sport utility vehicles, and trucks equipped with PSAN airbag inflators produced by Takata. Said campaign has an American Honda Motor counterpart now, and it's a big one.\nPer the recall documentation published by the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration , a whopping 810,592 vehicles from the Honda and Acura brands are being recalled stateside. The population includes models fitted with Takata airbag inflators that contain moisture-absorbing material. In a similar fashion to the Toyota recall , this one boils down to long-term propellant degradation.\nThe propellant can go bad when exposed to elevated temperatures, high humidity, and repeated temperature changes. In this context, degradation means the phase-stabilized ammonium nitrate propellant can burn abnormally when the airbag deploys, creating excessive pressure in the inflator housing. This, in turn, sends very sharp metal fragments through the airbag's cushion into the cabin.\n\"That can't happen to me,\" is what many would say, but do remember that iffy Takata inflators have taken the lives of more than 35 people worldwide. In the US, the NHTSA Takata Recall Spotlight cites 28 confirmed fatalities and more than 400 injuries nationwide since 2009. So yes, those inflators are really dangerous.\nAmerican Honda Motor's documentation confirms that affected inflators feature PSAN propellant and something called 13X, namely a desiccant intended to control moisture within the inflator. Despite the presence of 13X, the propellant may not perform as intended over time, fracturing more than intended. This increases the burning surface area, resulting in a higher rate of gas generation.\nPhoto: Honda American Honda Motor is recalling the 2012 through 2013 model year CR-V and Civic, 2013 Accord, 2013 Acura RDX, and the 2013 ILX. The recall population is restricted to vehicles that AHM dealers originally sold or customers registered in 14 states and territories with high temperatures and elevated humidity. California, Louisiana, Texas, Mississippi, and Georgia are some of them.\nThe rest are South Carolina, Alabama, Florida, Puerto Rico, Hawaii, Guam, the Northern Mariana Islands, American Samoa, and the US Virgin Islands. As you'd expect, the Civic accounts for the bulk of the recall population. Honda's compact is listed with 413,125 potentially affected cars, while the CR-V and Accord populations are estimated at 197,045 and 165,225 vehicles.\nAcura contributes 20,554 examples of the RDX and 14,643 of the ILX, amounting to a grand total of 810,592 automobiles. American Honda Motor has received no reports of injuries (or worse) associated with these inflators, making this a preventive recall based on degradation analysis. The investigation stretches back to June 2020 and an analysis of desiccated PSAN inflators.\nAHM found no signs of deterioration at that point, yet its statistical modeling indicated that propellant deterioration would advance far enough to create an inflator rupture risk before 2027 at the earliest. The investigation continued through November 2026, with AHM confirming that the inflators still operated within normal parameters.\nOut of an abundance of caution, both the front driver- and passenger-side inflators will be replaced free of charge with new units from a different supplier, complete with a different propellant chemistry that avoids long-term degradation problems under similar conditions. Interim notification letters will be mailed on December 18, 2026. Another notification will follow once sufficient parts become available.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda Civic honda cr-v Honda Accord Honda Acura Takata recall airbag\nDownload: Honda and Acura Takata airbag inflators November 2026 recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2027 Hyundai Elantra & 2028 Elantra Hybrid: How Do They Compare to Other Affordable Compact Sedans?",
